arriving in kabul by air is always  breathtaking  the scenery is spectacular the afghan  capital is surrounded by mountains it  can be a difficult landing even for the  most experienced pilot  the airport is large it's been  completely renovated by the  international community in the last 20  years  it was one of nato and the us's main air  bases before they pulled out their  forces in recent months it's now used by  civilian airlines and the afghan  military  and for the un and other aid agencies  it's a vital lifeline as roads have  become more dangerous supplies need to  be delivered by air from kabul  but now control of the airport has  become one of the most pressing  diplomatic issues  the us and other western embassies still  have large numbers of staff in the  country working in heavily fortified  compounds  turkish president recep tayyip erdogan  is offering to supply troops so the  airport remains secure and in a worst  case scenario can be used for an  evacuation of international diplomats  it's really important because the  airport is uh obviously the only way out  of the country and if diplomatic  missions are to stay in an afghanistan  that is going to be  administered at some level maybe in a  power sharing agreement by the taliban  then they  they need to be able to have a way out  if either a civil war starts or if the  taliban decide to gain more power and so  the  diplomatic activity of the country and  also the humanitarian aid of the country  is going to be decided by uh you know  whether the airport stays open or not so  far the taliban has flatly rejected the  idea of a turkish presence and i'm told  by diplomats that if a deal isn't done  soon the u.s is contemplating taking  control of the airport itself that would  be a breach of the deal it did with the  taliban last year and would complicate  things in this country still further  james bay's al jazeera kabul
 

